nodecode-handoff is a rewrite of yuzushi-dev's yuzushi-dev/hermes-handoff:

  https://github.com/yuzushi-dev/hermes-handoff
  commit 2bab6cf77a436544f58f5b528cd0d838e6d60309
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Daniele Verì (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the canonical v1 state (goal, constraints and preferences, progress
as done / in progress / pending, key decisions, rejected attempts,
verification, critical context, uncertainties, next steps) with its validation,
the rendering into the one six-section markdown page the original produced,
the secret scrub applied at the persistence boundary, the immutable,
project-bound store with a handoff:// reference, one filing per request id
(the same id with other content refused, not overwritten), reading a document
back with its section check, listing the store, and the provenance line that
says a document is the model's account and that reading it is not proof of a
successful resume. The markdown page keeps the original's order and its
folding of the rejected attempts, the verification and the uncertainties into
Critical Context.

Left out: the three things that were Hermes runtime rather than handoff. The
evidence action read a bounded, redacted tail of the persisted transcript out
of Hermes' own session database; Nodecode has no public reader of its session
store for a cell, and the model in hand already holds the conversation. The
opener -- the handoff-open CLI command, the Desktop pane, prepare_open,
opening_status and the durable attempt journal that made one launch at most --
opened a new Hermes session with its first prompt; a Nodecode session is the
person's own to open, and /handoff plus the reference does the rest by hand.
The optional writer model (summarize, with a model and provider of its own,
through the host's LLM facade) has no seam here either, and it was the
original's non-default path. Also left out: the profile state.db route
observer on the pre_api_request hook, which only fed the opener.
